course: dictionary
lesson: 8296
title: Rivalry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A rivalry is when people, teams or groups compete with each other for the same thing, often for a long time. The word comes from rival, which means someone you are up against. «Derbies are games between local rivals» tells you that rivals are the teams who play each other again and again.
explain: A rivalry can be friendly or fierce. A «friendly rivalry» is competing for fun, like two classes racing to read the most books. A «fierce rivalry» is strong and hard, and people may get angry. The words around rivalry, such as joke, dangerous or banter, are clues that tell you which kind it is.
explain: Rivalry can also mean jealous feelings between people, such as «sibling rivalry», when brothers and sisters compete for attention. Rivalry can make you try harder, but «competing to crush someone hurts», so it can also cause unkindness. Always check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence.
fact: Rivalry means competing with someone for the same thing, often for a long time.
fact: «Sibling rivalry» means brothers and sisters competing for attention.
fact: A rival is someone you compete with, like a team that you play again and again.
fact: «Rivalry is fine as banter, not as fights or hate.»
fact: Rivalry can push people to do better, but it can also cause unkindness.
exercise: «Derbies are games between local rivals.» What is a rival? | Someone who is always your best friend | Someone who never plays any games | Someone who lives far away | *Someone you compete with
exercise: What does «sibling rivalry» mean? | *Brothers and sisters competing for attention | Two cousins who never meet | Brothers and sisters who share a bedroom | A family holiday by the sea
exercise: «Rivalry can make you try harder, but competing to crush someone hurts.» What does this tell you? | Rivalry only happens in sport | Rivalry is always bad | *Rivalry can help or hurt, depending on how people treat each other | Rivalry means you must win every game
test: «A joke school rivalry has turned dangerous.» What made it turn dangerous? | Walking home together | Cheering each other on | Sharing a joke | *Throwing things at each other
test: What does «fierce rivalry» mean? | A type of football shirt | A rivalry that has already ended | *A strong, hard competition | A gentle and quiet friendship
exam: «Stepping out often makes a rivalry fizzle.» What does it mean for a rivalry to fizzle? | *Slowly fade away and stop | Win a prize | Grow into a huge fight | Get louder and stronger
